{
  "slug": "lebesgue-measure-oq-04-oq-01",
  "title": "Formalize the Cantor Function (Devil's Staircase)",
  "phase": "OBSERVE",
  "status": "available",
  "tier": "B",
  "path": "full",
  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\exists\\, F : [0,1] \\to [0,1] \\text{ continuous, monotone, surjective, constant on each removed middle-third interval, with } F' = 0 \\text{ Lebesgue-a.e., yet } F(1) - F(0) = 1.",
    "plain": "The Cantor function, the 'devil's staircase,' is continuous and increasing on [0,1], climbing from 0 to 1, yet it is flat on every open middle-third interval removed in the Cantor construction. Its entire rise is carried on the Cantor set, a set of Lebesgue measure zero, so F' = 0 almost everywhere even though F increases by a full unit. It is the canonical singular continuous function and is currently absent from Mathlib.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "It is the standard counterexample separating monotone continuity from absolute continuity: the fundamental theorem of calculus fails since the integral of F' is 0 while F(1) - F(0) = 1.",
      "It is the concrete witness of a nonzero singular measure supported on a Lebesgue-null set, seeding the theory of singular distributions and self-affine functions.",
      "It completes the gallery's Cantor-set story: the parent proof shows the set is an uncountable null set; this builds the function whose entire variation lives on that null set."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [],
    "open": [
      "No construction of the Cantor function F : ℝ → ℝ (or on [0,1]) exists in Mathlib or this gallery.",
      "No formal proof that such an F is continuous, monotone, and surjective onto [0,1], nor constant on each removed middle-third interval.",
      "No formal proof that F' = 0 Lebesgue-almost-everywhere, nor the singularity statement that the integral of F' is 0 while F(1) - F(0) = 1."
    ],
    "goal": "Construct F and prove its defining properties (continuity, monotonicity, surjectivity onto [0,1], constancy on each removed middle-third interval), then establish F' = 0 Lebesgue-a.e. Stage one: construction plus continuity/monotonicity/surjectivity; stage two: the a.e.-derivative-zero result."
  },
